{"ex-3A02-1":{"number":"1","title":"Applying Snell’s law","question":"<p>A light ray enters medium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from air as shown.</p><p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 15°, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 10°.</p><p>(a) Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> .</p><p>(b) Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 60°.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1575f09c744aaa6884ef8cd3.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:142.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>˚</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>˚</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.49</p><p>(b) By Snell’s law,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>˚</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>˚</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>˚</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 35.5°</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":2},"id":"ex-3A02-1","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-2":{"number":"2","title":"Refractive index","question":"<p>Connie directs a ray of light from glass to air (Fig a). She then plots a graph of sin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> against sin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> (Fig b).</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f9d26056d0b3fdf4934e0bfc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:460.3px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of the glass.</p><p>(b) Connie directs a ray of light from medium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to air. The refractive index of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is smaller than that of glass. Sketch the graph of sin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> against sin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in Figure b.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = slope = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.5</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f54c95866f46bde7f171122f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:183.6px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The slope of the graph instead of one data point is used to calculate n. This averages out the error in each measurement. Daily-life media have refractive indices greater than 1. You should check your answer if you have n &lt; 1.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>∴ n = slope</p><p>n ↓ ⇒ slope ↓</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 10","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":14},"id":"ex-3A02-2","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-3":{"number":"3","title":"General form of Snell’s law","question":"<p>A ray of light travels from glass to medium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The refractive index of glass is 1.50. Find the angle of refraction if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is</p><p>(a) air;</p><p>(b) water (refractive index = 1.33);</p><p>(c) oil (refractive index = 1.47).</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/cc062a6877ea815695d56180.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:151.9px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>θ<sub>g</sub> = 90° – 60° = 30°</p><p>Apply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>g</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>X</sub>.</p><p>(a) 1.50 × sin 30° = 1 × sin θ<sub>a</sub></p><p>θ<sub>a</sub> = 48.6°</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/81bca9cc716a71a6e8eeffe7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:151.9px\"/></p><p>(b) 1.50 × sin 30° = 1.33 × sin θ<sub>w</sub></p><p>θ<sub>w</sub> = 34.3°</p><p>(c) 1.50 × sin 30° = 1.47 × sin θ<sub>o</sub></p><p>θ<sub>o</sub> = 30.7°</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 12","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":30},"id":"ex-3A02-3","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-4":{"number":"4","title":"Finding the speed of light in different media","question":"<p>(a) The speed of light in air is 3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>–1</sup> and the refractive index of water is 1.33. Find the speed of light in water.</p><p>(b) When a light ray travels from medium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to medium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, its speed changes from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The angle of incidence and the angle of</p><p>refraction are θ<sub>X</sub> and θ<sub>Y</sub> respectively. Show that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Speed of light in water <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2.26 × 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>–1</sup></p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>X</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>Y</sub></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × sin θ<sub>X</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × sin θ<sub>Y</sub></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 15","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":47},"id":"ex-3A02-4","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-5":{"number":"5","title":"Speed of light in different media","question":"<p>Light travels through media <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> as shown. The boundaries between the media are parallel.</p><p>(a) Which medium has the smallest refractive index? Explain.</p><p>(b) In which medium does light travel the fastest? Explain.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/ca6dca9966b783c6805d98a9.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:163.9px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>X</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>Y</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>Z</sub>,</p><p>θ<sub>X</sub> &gt; θ<sub>Z</sub> &gt; θ<sub>Y</sub> ⇒ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> &lt; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> &lt; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>∴   <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has the smallest refractive index.</p><p>(b) Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> &lt; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> &lt; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> ⇒ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> &gt; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> &gt; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>∴   Light travels the fastest in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 16","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":57},"id":"ex-3A02-5","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-6":{"number":"6","title":"Total internal reflection","question":"<p>Water has a refractive index of 1.33.</p><p>(a) Find the critical angle of a water-air interface.</p><p>(b) Will total internal reflection occur in the following cases?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/7dafd781a7023f9870ed5052.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:400.4px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) sin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 48.8°</p><p>(b) (i) Yes</p><p>(ii) No</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>i &gt; C</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Total internal reflection never occurs when light travels from an optically less dense medium</p><p>(air) to an optically denser medium (water).</p>","think":"<p>Will the answers to (b) change if glass of refractive index 1.5 is used instead of water?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>No. The glass–air critical angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. In (i), light travels from glass to air with incidence angle 50°, greater than the critical angle, so total internal reflection still occurs. In (ii), light travels from air into glass, so total internal reflection still cannot occur.</p>","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 22","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":70},"id":"ex-3A02-6","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-7":{"number":"7","title":"Light ray through a block (I)","question":"<p>A ray of light hits a glass block as shown. Sketch the path of the light ray.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/bf3e5a0b75c855d4c41b0d16.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:200.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a7e79a45e22811461eb06910.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:223.6px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>n = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.62</p><p>sin C = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>C = 38.1°</p><p>At side AB,</p><p>i = 90° – 32.3°</p><p>= 57.7°</p><p>&gt; 38.1°</p><p>∴ T otal internal reflection occurs.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 23","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":91},"id":"ex-3A02-7","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-8":{"number":"8","title":"Mirage","question":"<p>A ray of light from the sky travels through parallel layers of air of different refractive indices as shown.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/0a2068ab5f27cdbee1a8d77a.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:525.5px\"/></p><p>(a) Find θ<sub>1</sub> if θ<sub>5</sub> = 90°. It is given that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 1.000 273 and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 1.000 233.</p><p>(b) The observer sees the image of the sky on the ground in front of him. If he walks forwards, can he get closer to the image? Explain.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>1</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>2</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>3</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>4</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>5</sub></p><p>sin θ<sub>1</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>5</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> sin 90°</p><p>θ<sub>1</sub> = 89.5°</p><p>(b) No, this is because the image is formed by the reflection of light from the sky at the same fixed angle even if he walks forwards.</p><p>Revision 2 Q28 (p.75)</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 26","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":108},"id":"ex-3A02-8","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-9":{"number":"9","title":"Radius of a diver’s view","question":"<p>A diver underwater looks upwards. His view is squeezed into a circle. What is the radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of this circle? His eyes are 0.5 m below the water surface. The refractive index of water is 1.33.</p>","answer":"<p>sin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 48.75°</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.5 tan 48.75° = 0.570 m</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/dfb0740440ed61dfa9b6724f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:239.1px\"/></p>","tips":"","think":"<p>If the diver is at a deeper position, how will θ and R change?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>The viewing angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> remains unchanged because it is determined by the critical angle, which depends only on the refractive indices. The half-angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; the full cone angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The radius increases: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>tan</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, so it is proportional to the depth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 27","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":120},"id":"ex-3A02-9","chapter":"3A02"},"ex-3A02-10":{"number":"10","title":"Use of cladding in optical fibres","question":"<p>A simple optical fibre is made of a plastic core with no outer cladding. The refractive index of the plastic is 1.62. A light ray is incident normally from one end of the fibre and</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/4118f4903d28eb437be86ff7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:192.8px\"/></p><p>reaches point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> as shown.</p><p>(a) A dirt patch of refractive index 1.7 is accidentally attached to the outside of the optical fibre at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Explain whether total internal reflection will occur or not when the ray reaches point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) If the core is coated with a cladding of refractive index 1.52, will total internal reflection occur when the ray reaches point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Total internal reflection will not occur. The dirt has a greater refractive index than the core, so light is refracted out to the dirt.</p><p>(b) By Snell’s law,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>1</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>2</sub></p><p>1.62 × sin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1.52 × sin 90°</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = sin<sup>–1</sup> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>) = 69.8°</p><p>Since the critical angle is smaller than 70°, total internal reflection will occur when the ray reaches point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>Exercise 2.2 Q4 (p.66)</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3A  |  Chapter 3A02  |  Source PDF p. 29","source":{"name":"3A02 Examples.docx","sha256":"f54277b8314bee3fd55c8698782006ba5ae923411de4d1b7e8a5cd1a378aef04","block":132},"id":"ex-3A02-10","chapter":"3A02"}}